/* 2026-08-08: hours band loses its fill — owner pick G from a seven-option sheet rendered on the real card. The band had carried #fdf8f0, which is the SAME HEX as the .day-block behind the stop card (page #f5f4f0, day #fdf8f0, card #f5f0e6, band #fdf8f0). A fill identical to the layer two levels behind it does not read as a strip laid on the card, it reads as a hole cut through it - which is why the several earlier attempts to fix the look by changing the RAIL colour all missed: the rail was never the problem. No fill, rail and ink to brand terracotta #b85c2a / #7a3b1e, chevron chip to the same pair with a transparent fill, both dark paths to transparent. The change carries a spacing consequence that is easy to miss and was caught by measuring: a tint's ink is its BOX EDGE, a text row's is its TEXT, so with the fill gone the band must be spaced as the ordinary row it now is. Its padding drops to 0 and every margin returns to the card default (6px, with nothing special at first/last row) - left on the tint-model 9.125/3.125 values it measured 18.25 against the card's 12.25 constant. Re-verified with Brain/scripts/gap_audit.py: every in-scope pair 12.13-12.38. gap_audit.py itself was fixed in the same pass - it treated a painted container as a leaf, so the moment the band lost its fill the walk stopped at the card and reported card gaps while still looking clean. Locked by contract 8 of check_stop_hours_contract, which now also fails on any restored fill. toolbar.js -> v259. CACHE to v398. */
/* 2026-08-09: Floating back pill was one size inside a guide and another size everywhere else — owner report, mobile. The back pill (#tve-back-to-guide / #tve-back-to-byg), the history pills (#tve-nav-back / #tve-nav-fwd), the days pill (.day-jump-btn) and the scroll-top FAB (.tve-scroll-top) each declare a fixed height in toolbar.js / guide-style.css — 34px for the pills, 36px for the FAB. mobile.css § 7 sets a blanket min-height:40px tap target on a/button/select/textarea, and min-height ALWAYS beats height in the box model regardless of specificity, so that 40px floor silently overrode all six. Guides do not load mobile.css and every one of the 104 non-guide pages does, which is exactly why the size changed with the page: 34px inside a guide, 40px on Trip-Essentials/Best-Of/maps. It also stretched the 36px round FAB into a 36x40 oval off-guide. Fix is one exemption block in mobile.css setting min-height:auto on those six selectors — no height is declared there, each control still owns its own, so the shared 23px-above-the-floor optical line is untouched. Verified by measuring getBoundingClientRect at 393px across all 105 non-guide pages plus a 12-guide sample, from a guide referrer and a Before-You-Go referrer: one bucket per control, back pill 34px everywhere (103 guide-sourced + 23 BYG-sourced), FAB 36px everywhere. mobile.css -> v69. CACHE to v399. */
/* 2026-08-09: Floating pill family widened to the two pills the first pass missed — #tve-map-back (the "‹ All Maps" back pill on the 8 continent/world map pages, authored 34px) and #tve-bo-jump (the "🌍 N countries" jump pill on the 32 Best-Of pages). Both were being flattened to 40px by the same mobile.css § 7 tap-target floor, so a map page showed a 34px "← {City}" back pill and a 40px "‹ All Maps" back pill side by side. Exempting them uncovered a drift the floor had been hiding: #tve-bo-jump was authored at 36px/18px-radius against the family's 34px/17px, invisible while both were flattened to 40 — corrected to 34/17 in the same pass. The Best-Of FAB lift `body.tve-has-bo-jump .tve-scroll-top` was computed off the inflated height (20 + 40 + 12 = 72) and now uses the real one (20 + 34 + 12 = 66); measured back at exactly 12px of clearance. All eight controls are pinned by brain_check.check_mobile_floating_controls_tap_target_exempt (Cleanliness rule 738). toolbar.js -> v260. CACHE to v400. */
/* 2026-08-09: "Also a day trip from" row joins the Trip Overview table instead of hanging under it — owner report. The row was authored as a standalone 12px muted sentence with 9px 16px 10px padding and a flex-wrap city list, while every day row above it had since been re-rendered by _dayRowRail as a three-column table: a 58px uppercase gold rail carrying "DAY N", a 14px gap, then the body at the day-title size. The result was a line that shared neither the left edge, the type size nor the colour of the rows it belonged to, which is exactly why it read as odd. The injected #tve-adtf-css now mirrors the railed row exactly — flex, gap 14px, padding 10px 16px, body at var(--fs-overview-day-title), rail label at 11px/700/.08em in --c-brand — and the ≤600px block mirrors the day row's stacked grid form (label on line one, body full width) rather than only shrinking the padding. Measured at 1280px and 393px on Budapest, Bologna and Munich: label left and body left match the last day row's .ovd-num / .ovd-stops to the pixel (64/136 desktop, 46/46 mobile), same 11px and 15px. The rail label no longer carries the sentence — it carries the row's KIND, "ALSO", the way the rows above carry "DAY 6"; the sentence moved into the body where it opens like a Train Day body ("🚆 · Day trip from Vienna"), so the emoji column lines up too. The row stays a <div>, so the day rows' <a>-based :nth-of-type zebra is untouched. check_day_trip_from_theme_tokens moved with the design: it now pins --c-brand on the rail label and --c-text-muted on the body, smoke-tested to fail on a hardcoded hex. toolbar.js -> v261. CACHE to v401. */
/* 2026-08-09: "Also a day trip from" row adopts the day-row wording outright — owner rule: "lets write Also or Extra below day 1, 2 then the rest of the format follow what we use already. Icon Train day etc". The row had kept its own sentence ("Day trip from Vienna") after the geometry pass aligned it to the rail; the label column now carries the row KIND ("ALSO", the way the rows above carry "DAY 6") and the body is a Train Day body verbatim — same 🚆, same "Train Day", same dot before the city — so the two lines differ in one word rather than in phrasing. That word is "from", and it is not decoration: this row is the INBOUND view (other guides run a train day HERE), so a bare "🚆 · Train Day · Vienna" on the Budapest guide would read as a train day TO Vienna and state the exact opposite of the data. Rendered and read back at 1280px and 393px on Budapest (one city) and Bologna (three): "ALSO | 🚆 · Train Day from · Vienna" sitting directly under "DAY 6 | 🚆 · Train Day · Eger…" on the same left edge and type size. toolbar.js -> v262. CACHE to v402. */
/* 2026-08-09: Trip Overview day rail reaches dark mode — the table was unthemed and nobody had looked at it in dark. _dayRowRail turned the day rows into a three-column table, but both of its colours were written as bare hex inside their own rules: .ovd-stops kept color:#6f695d and the zebra kept background:#fbf8f2. Neither is a token, so the dark block could not reach them — the stop names ran at 2.42:1 on the #2a2825 card (text you cannot read) and every EVEN row painted a cream #fbf8f2 band straight across the dark table, the single most visible thing on the page. Tokenised as --c-ovd-stops / --c-ovd-zebra, light values unchanged so light mode is pixel-identical (verified: rgb(111,105,93) and rgb(251,248,242) before and after). Dark: #b0aaa0 text at 6.37:1 on the card and 5.77:1 on the zebra, and the zebra becomes #322f2b — one step UP from --c-card-bg, the same lift --c-pill-hover uses, because a table reads as one surface with a raised alternate row rather than a punched-out darker one. toolbar.js's injected "ALSO" row now reads --c-ovd-stops itself instead of the near-match --c-text-muted, so its body colour is byte-identical to the rows above it in BOTH themes and cannot drift when either moves; check_day_trip_from_theme_tokens pins that token, smoke-tested against a scratchpad copy. Measured in both schemes on Budapest. NOT changed, and left for the owner: .ovd-count ("4 stops") is #9a9284 with no dark value and reads 3.08:1 in LIGHT — below AA — but darkening it to pass would flatten the hierarchy that keeps the count quieter than the stop names, which is a design call, not a bug fix. guide-style.css -> v150, toolbar.js -> v263. CACHE to v403. */
/* 2026-08-09: The last control on the floating row — #visited-pill-mobile, the cream "Visited" toggle on World-Map.html. It was the only member of the family with NO declared height at all: sized purely by padding, so mobile.css § 7's 40px tap-target floor set its height outright rather than merely overriding one, and it sat 6px taller than the "‹ All Guides" back pill directly beside it on the same row — visible in a single screenshot once the other eight were corrected. Given the family's explicit height:34px (padding 8px 20px -> 0 20px; the 20px radius is left alone, it clamps to 17 at this height and the cream/gold skin is deliberately distinct from the white/gold nav pills) and added to the mobile.css exemption. The seven region maps are redirect stubs to World-Map.html, so this one inline style covers all eight map URLs. All nine controls now measure 34px (36px for the round FAB) on guides, Trip-Essentials, Best-Of and the maps alike. Pinned by brain_check.check_mobile_floating_controls_tap_target_exempt (Cleanliness rule 738). mobile.css -> v71. CACHE to v404. */
/* 2026-08-09: Open/closed stopped being the reader's decision — owner rule: "it should tell me if it is open or not period. it cant be my decision". The Trip Overview row carried a green toggle pill labelled "Open right now" which, when switched on, dimmed every currently-closed stop, stamped a red Closed badge on it and auto-collapsed Day 2+ behind a "filter active" note. Two faults, one root: the label was phrased as a statement of FACT, so a clickable pill made no sense (owner: "confused what is the use of this pill and why i can change if open or not"), and a status the reader has to switch on is not a status at all. The pill, the dimming, the auto-collapse and the filter note are all gone; every stop whose hours parse now carries a plain Open / Closed badge in its .stop-header, always, refreshed each minute against the DESTINATION's clock. Two silences are deliberate, because a wrong badge is worse than none: no resolvable timezone -> no badge at all (never judge the destination by the reader's own clock), and unparseable or absent hours -> no badge. The clock pill stays and now earns its place — it is the reference time the badges are computed against. _injectOpenNowFilter renamed _injectOpenNowStatus; .open-now-pill/.open-now-dot/.stop-closed/.closed-now-badge/.day-header-filter-note all deleted, replaced by .open-now-status.is-open/.is-closed in both themes. Verified by rendering Melbourne (2:29am local: 16 Closed, 10 Open — all ten "Open 24/7") and Geneva (6:29pm: 9 Open, 4 Closed), zero toggles, zero unstyled badges, no console errors. guide-style.css -> v151, toolbar.js -> v264. CACHE to v405. */
/* 2026-08-09: ALSO row — the departure city stops being gold, and the row finally takes its turn in the white/beige rhythm (owner report, both on one screenshot of Salzburg). (1) The city was a gold link (--c-index-accent) sitting in the same column as "Berchtesgaden" and "Königssee" on the row above, which are plain body text — same kind of thing, two different colours, so it read as a foreign object rather than the next line of the table. Now color:inherit, which ties it to --c-ovd-stops in BOTH themes for free; it reveals itself on hover (terracotta + underline) instead of by shouting. No :visited rule is needed — .tve-adtf a is a class-specificity author selector and already outranks the UA visited style in every state. (2) The zebra never reached this row and could not: guide-style.css tints .overview-day.tve-railed:nth-of-type(even), which counts <a> siblings, and the ALSO row is a <div> — deliberately, so it does not shift the day rows' own parity. The stripe therefore just stopped, and on any guide with an ODD number of days two white rows ended up stacked (Salzburg: DAY 8 beige, DAY 9 white, ALSO white). Now stamped in JS by _dtfZebra, which reads the parity off the LAST DAY ROW (last.matches(":nth-of-type(even)")) rather than computing it from days.length — the CSS counts elements of the same TYPE, so any future <a> inside the section would shift the count, and asking the row itself cannot be wrong. It runs in rAF because _dayRowRail stamps .tve-railed — the class the zebra rule requires — from a DOMContentLoaded listener registered AFTER this one, so the class is not there yet at insert time; the guard on .tve-railed also means a guide whose rows never railed correctly gets no stripe. Verified in both schemes on four guides across both parities — Salzburg (9 days) and Annecy (3) take the beige, Budapest (6) and Bologna (4) stay white, and link/body/.ovd-stops resolve to one identical colour in every case. brain_check now pins color:inherit on the link and --c-ovd-zebra on the tint. toolbar.js -> v265. CACHE to v406. */
/* 2026-08-09: CSS half of the open-now status landed separately — the JS half (toolbar.js v264) was swept into e6cda230 by a parallel crib's commit and reached origin without its stylesheet, so guides were briefly emitting .open-now-status badges against a sheet that still defined only the retired .closed-now-badge. guide-style.css now carries .open-now-status.is-open/.is-closed in both themes plus the =<600px rule that drops the badge onto its own line (a zero-height flex-basis:100% .stop-header::after forces the wrap; .stop-header is nowrap and .stop-name is flex:0 1 auto/min-width:0, so an inline badge shrank the NAME and grew headers up to 79px on Bologna at 393px). Measured 25px uniform growth on mobile, 0px desktop, no overflow. guide-style.css -> v152. CACHE to v407. */
/* 2026-08-09: Two back pills were stacked on World-Map. #tve-map-back ("← All Guides") and #tve-back-to-guide ("← {City}") both seat at bottom:24px / left:16px, so a reader who reached the map FROM a guide got both in the same spot — same z-index:1400, and the map pill injects later, so it painted over the contextual one and the return-to-guide path was invisible and unclickable. Pre-existing, not a side effect of the floating-pill size work: neither pill's position was ever touched, and forcing both back to their old 40px height reproduces the identical collision. _injectMapBackPill() now returns early when #tve-back-to-guide or #tve-back-to-byg is already in the DOM — the same principle that already excludes per-guide stops-maps (where the page already carries a back control, this pill does not add a second). The map pill is the one that yields because "← {City}" returns the reader to the exact guide card they left, which nothing else on the page offers, while "← All Guides" duplicates a destination the hamburger CLOSE tap already reaches; owner rule 2026-08-06 also requires every page reachable from a guide to show the back pill. Reading the DOM is safe rather than a race: injectBackToGuidePill() is an IIFE ~5,300 lines earlier, so its build() has already run (parsed document) or its DOMContentLoaded listener was registered first (loading). Verified on all three arrival paths at 393px — from a guide: "← Abu Dhabi" alone; direct/bookmark and from Before-You-Go: "← All Guides" alone; zero overlaps in every case. Locked by brain_check.check_map_back_pill. toolbar.js -> v266. CACHE to v408. */
/* 2026-08-09: Open/Closed stop status drops the pill — owner pick D from a five-option sheet rendered on the real Melbourne header. It had shipped as a filled 10px badge sitting directly beside the .stop-dur chip: same shape, same weight, so it read as a second CONTROL in a row that already has one — the exact confusion the retired toggle caused, reintroduced smaller (owner: "i think the pill is too big there"). A statement is not a button and must not be shaped like one, so fill, border and padding all go and colour alone carries the state, leaving .stop-dur the only pill in the row. Dark mode could not reuse the on-tint #6dff9e — as bare text on the #2a2825 card it reads neon — so open takes #a8d8b8, the same palette green one step calmer, at 9.22:1; closed keeps #fca5a5 at 7.74:1. Light is 6.21:1 / 7.71:1. No new colours. The =<600px own-line rule STAYS, and the three-way render is why: inline at 393px shreds a long name (Bologna's MAMbo went to seven one-word lines), while wrap lets .stop-name take its natural width instead of being crushed by flex:0 1 auto/min-width:0 on a nowrap row. Measured against the pre-badge original, per-header delta runs -33px to +21px — long names come out SHORTER than they were — and desktop is exactly 0px. guide-style.css -> v153. CACHE to v409. */
/* 2026-08-09: Forward pill deleted and the whole floating family cut a size — owner, mobile, on a guide ("we dont need a forward pill do we?" / "all these 3 pills are too big in size also"). #tve-nav-fwd shipped bottom-right, hidden until a popstate revealed it; the browser's own forward gesture already covers that case and a third control crowded a row that has 393px to seat Back, the days pill, the arrow and a thumb. Removed at source (toolbar.js), from the mobile.css tap-target exemption list, from _MOBILE_FLOATING_CONTROLS in brain_check, and from Toolbar.html / Validator Index / Cleanliness rules 725 + 738 in the same pass. Size: the family went 34px -> 28px with 13px -> 12px text and 17px -> 14px radius, the round FAB 36px -> 30px, all mobile-only — the desktop .day-jump-btn / .tve-scroll-top base rules stay 36px. One size everywhere still holds: all five injected pills, the days pill, the Visited toggle and the FAB moved together, so nothing re-splits the family the 2026-08-09 exemption pass just aligned. Two derived values re-tuned with the height rather than left stale: the Best-Of FAB lift body.tve-has-bo-jump .tve-scroll-top from 66 to 60 (20 + 28 + 12) and MOBILE_FLOAT_BAND_PX from 41 to 35 (bottom:5 + 30), the floor the mobile .container bottom padding must clear — the shipped 72px padding clears both. Pills seat at bottom:6px and the FAB at bottom:5px, so all three centres land on one line 20px above the viewport floor, down from 23px. toolbar.js -> v267, guide-style.css -> v154, mobile.css -> v72, web-travel-style.css -> v17. CACHE to v410. */
/* 2026-08-09: Comment-only bump — the .hotel-first / .arrive-first block in guide-style.css was still teaching two banned glyphs. It documented the day opener as `From Hotel` (hotel glyph U+1F3E8, banned 2026-08-08) and the train-day arrival as `ARRIVE {station}` (station glyph U+1F689, banned 2026-08-09), both hard-failed by validate_itinerary.py. The comment now states the rule it should have stated all along (motion banners lead with words, never a glyph) and names both retired codepoints as banned rather than current. Found by the new brain_check.check_no_station_emoji_in_site on its first run against the real tree — which is exactly why that check scans CSS comments and the arrow ban does not: the U+1F392 backpack precedent (2026-08-08) was a retired glyph left in a comment in THIS file that kept feeding itself back into mockups. No selector, rule or value changed, so nothing renders differently; the bump exists solely because the shared-asset push gate is mechanical and a stale-comment fix that never reaches installed PWAs is a fix that did not ship. guide-style.css -> v155. CACHE to v411. */
/* 2026-08-09: Stop-type filter chips at the foot of the Trip Overview on every guide (#tve-stf) — All plus one chip per stop type actually present in that guide, each with its count. Picking one hides every non-matching .stop-block, drops any .day-block left with no visible stop, hides the .next / .next-tram / .next-metro / .hotel-first / .arrive-first route lines (a hop pointing at a filtered-out stop is nonsense, and the walking times between the stops that remain are not the authored ones) and dims the Trip Overview rows for hidden days. A collapsed day holding matches opens for the filter and re-collapses on All; days are never auto-collapsed. Never persisted — every load starts on All, because a reader returning to a guide missing two thirds of its stops reads that as a broken page, not as their own filter. The classifier reads the stop NAME, not an emoji: there is no stop-type emoji in a guide (16 of 2,932 .stop-header elements carry any emoji at all, and all 16 are part of the place name), so it matches an accent-folded head-noun lexicon plus compound suffixes (Vondelpark, Rijksmuseum, Fernsehturm), falls back to multi-word description phrases, then to a Landmarks bucket. Gated on 4+ stops and 2+ populated buckets, so Orlando (3 stops) gets no row; 236 of 237 guides do. CSS is self-injected as #tve-stf-css — the self-styled variant, guarded by brain_check.check_toolbar_self_styled_classes — so guide-style.css is untouched and needs no bump. toolbar.js -> v268. CACHE to v412. */
