/* 2026-08-10: OWNER — mobile and desktop are separated again. Every 600/601/1260 breakpoint on the site now pairs viewport width with the PRIMARY POINTER, because width alone cannot tell a phone from a desktop window the user narrowed — both report 500px, so narrowing the browser collapsed the site into the mobile layout, hamburger and all ("somehow mobile got mixed with the desktop!" · "needs to hold"). Mobile is `and (pointer: coarse)`, desktop is `, (pointer: fine)` (a comma, i.e. OR, so it keeps applying in a narrow window), and the nav swap takes the same pairing at 1260px. A trackpad reports `fine` at ANY width, so the desktop layout holds all the way down; a touchscreen laptop stays desktop too, its primary pointer being the trackpad. 189 queries across 5 shared sheets and 59 Trip-Essentials pages; guide HTML needed none — guide-style.css carries it, so no HMAC stamp moves. JS gates route through the new window.TVE.isPhone(), which runs the identical query, so CSS and JS cannot drift. Intermediate reflow breakpoints (640/700/860/1100) stay on width alone by design — they reflow a grid without changing which world you are in. One defect this exposed and fixed: the tab strip is built to fit 1260px and the hamburger used to take over below that, so nothing had ever asked what happens at 700px on a mouse — it pushed the whole page sideways ~870px. .tb-inner regains overflow-x:auto, scoped to (max-width:1259px) and (pointer:fine) so Rule 582's no-sliding-toolbar guarantee at normal desktop width is untouched. Measured after: 0px page overflow at 500/900/1440px desktop and 393px phone. Toolbar.html Sec 42; Cleanliness 822. toolbar.js -> v339, guide-style.css -> v164, mobile.css -> v76, web-travel-style.css -> v24, guides-index-style.css -> v2. CACHE to v502. */
/* 2026-08-10: Hotels & Flights — airport PICKER on both Flights fields. Typing a place name was never validated, so the 'a code that does not exist wont complete' rule only ever covered code-shaped input: 'bahia' sailed through to a dead Google query (owner). Both fields now resolve to a real airport before the search can run — type anything, pick from the list, the field holds a code. Ranked by exact code, then the site's own 198 FMAP destinations/hubs, then OurAirports size tier, then city; without the middle two, 'paris' surfaced Le Bourget above CDG and 'seattle' put Boeing Field above SEA. New asset airport-names.json (3,268 airports, 165 KB) is fetched on FIRST KEYSTROKE in a Flights field, never on page load. Hotels destination deliberately stays free text — a hotel search wants a place, not an airport. toolbar.js -> v340, guide-style.css -> v165. CACHE to v503. */
/* 2026-08-10: OWNER — the theme toggle is pinned into the tab row as its LAST TAB ("pin in the page as if it was the last tab and when we reduce it will behave like the rest"). It used to be a sibling of .tb-inner, i.e. its own item in .tb's wrap context, and the tab row measures 1414px — so on ANY window under ~1500px the row filled line 1 and the toggle dropped to a centred line of its own underneath. That is an ordinary laptop, not just a narrowed window. As a child of .tb-links it wraps and reflows with the tabs and cannot separate from them. Below 1260px, where .tb-inner is display:none, it is re-parented back onto .tb — the shipped mobile arrangement, sun left / wordmark centre / hamburger right — and a matchMedia listener moves it live so dragging a window across the breakpoint needs no reload. Paired change: .tb-links may wrap between 1260px and 1499px on a fine pointer, so the row reflows to two lines instead of pushing the page sideways. And the 1260px NAV SWAP goes back to width-only, deliberately: page layout can hold in a narrow window because a card can be narrower, but a 1414px tab row cannot, and all three alternatives measured worse than the hamburger (toggle orphaned / a scroller showing a two-letter fragment of one tab / a 476px-tall wall of one tab per line). The hamburger on a narrow desktop window is responsive nav, not the mobile site - the page around it stays desktop, which is what "needs to hold" asked for. brain_check: the pointer-gating guard now covers 600/601 only, and Rule 582's <=1260px hamburger guard exempts `and(pointer:fine)` queries, which never swap the nav. Toolbar.html Sec 42. toolbar.js -> v341. CACHE to v504. */
/* 2026-08-10: OWNER — the stop header splits into facts-then-controls, and the open/closed status becomes a PILL. The duration chip carried margin-left:auto and floated to the far right, putting five things on the right of every header (status, duration, share, star, notes): "too much stuff on the right". How long a stop takes is a fact ABOUT the stop, like its name; share/star/notes are controls. The chip now sits against the title and is the row's spacer, so the row reads name -> ✓ -> duration -> status ......... controls. Status ORDER moved in the same pass: as bare text between the round ✓ and the round chip it read as compressed filler, so it now closes the group ("pill time comes first if not it needs a pill because look odd compressed in the middle"). Six treatments were mocked up at real size; the owner picked C — a state-coloured pill, fill and hairline being the state colour itself at 9%/45% (13%/40% in dark, since a pale ink over a dark card separates far less). That REVERSES pick D of 2026-08-09 ("a statement is not a button"), which did not survive the chip moving up against the title. line-height:1.92 makes the two pills exactly 25.2px, matching the chip's 12px x 1.6 content box. On a VISITED stop the status is REMOVED, not faded: whether a place is open now is live information for a stop you are deciding to walk to and means nothing for one you have already been to ("it does not make a lot of sense to see the open there"); the chip stays faded because how long it took is still true. Mobile keeps the pair together on line 2, chip first, same order as desktop — measured 1182px of header across Athens' 20 stops vs 1184px before, and far more uniform (was 47/60/61/72, now 56/70). 0px overflow at 393px and at 500/900/1440px desktop, light and dark. guide-style.css -> v166. CACHE to v505. */
/* 2026-08-10: OWNER — "center in the middle. no mobile hamburger on desktop!" Two changes. (1) The nav swap is pointer-gated again, so a DESKTOP window keeps its tab row at every width and the hamburger belongs to touch devices only. The row measures 1414px, so below ~1500px it wraps onto extra lines rather than scrolling or pushing the page - 500px gives four lines, 900px two, and the theme toggle rides along as the row's last tab exactly as asked yesterday. (2) Those wrapped lines are CENTRED (.tb-links justify-content:center inside the wrap query). The JS nav gate now mirrors the CSS query including the pointer term; without it the toggle was re-parented out of the tab row on a narrow desktop and stranded beside a wordmark with no menu. Two validators learned the difference: check_toolbar_centering bans justify-content:center on .tb-links because a row wider than its container overflows BOTH sides and hides the first tab - it now exempts a block that also declares flex-wrap:wrap, since a wrapping row has no horizontal overflow at all; and Rule 582's <=1260px hamburger ceiling exempts and(pointer:fine) queries, which never swap the nav. Measured: 0px page overflow at 500/700/900/1100/1320/1440/1700px desktop and 393px phone, hamburger absent at every desktop width. Toolbar.html Sec 42. toolbar.js -> v342. CACHE to v506. */
/* 2026-08-10: OWNER — the three stop-header action icons speak one language: outline = off, SOLID = on, and hover fills to show what clicking would give you ("when selecting should hover full terracotta like the bookmark so we know we selected"). Hover previously only darkened the stroke to #7a3b1e, a colour shift on a 1px outline that is invisible in practice; filling changes the glyph's whole silhouette, so it reads instantly and without colour vision. The STAR is the only true toggle of the three, so it alone previews the state you would land in: unsaved + hover fills, saved + hover EMPTIES back to an outline — without the inverse, "already on my list" and "about to be" were the same solid shape. Share is a one-shot action and the pencil opens an editor, so both simply fill; a stop that already has a note is solid at rest, so its hover darkens instead. One trap worth recording: the saved star swaps to a DIFFERENT svg (_starFill) whose <path> carries its own fill="currentColor" presentation attribute, so inheriting fill from the <svg> element — what every other icon rule here does — cannot reach it; the un-fill has to name `svg path`. Ships alongside the owner-directed dropdown row icons (NAV_ICONS -> .tb-ico), whose CSS and JS halves are both present. toolbar.js -> v343. CACHE to v507. */
